    [Feature] Add release note fragments workflow (#18819)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    This PR introduces a release note fragments workflow to streamline the
    release notes assembly process. Instead of manually analyzing the entire
    commit history at release time, contributors now declare user-facing
    changes as a YAML fragment in the same PR.
    
    Key changes:
    - Added `release_notes/` directory containing the fragment template
    (`TEMPLATE.yaml`), contributor guidelines (`README.md`), and an
    `archive/` directory for consumed fragments.
    - Added `collect_release_notes.py` script to validate, merge, and group
    fragment files into a release notes draft, audit commits for missing
    fragments, and archive consumed fragments.
    - Updated the PR template, contributor guide, `AGENTS.md`, and the
    release skill (including checklist templates) to document and integrate
    this new process.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    No, this is a developer-facing workflow and documentation update.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    The `collect_release_notes.py` script was tested locally with sample
    fragment files and raw commit lists to verify validation, merging,
    grouping, and archiving functionality.
    
    ---
    
    ### Checklist
    
    #### Testing
    
    - [ ] **Boundary / edge-case coverage**: I reviewed whether this change
    involves
    boundary conditions (e.g. empty input, single/zero element, min/max
    values,
    off-by-one, sequence-length limits, integer overflow). Where applicable,
    I
    added unit tests covering these edge cases in `tests/ut/` or
    `tests/e2e/`.
    - [ ] **Regression test**: If this PR fixes a bug, I added a regression
    test
      that would fail without the fix.
    - [ ] **Error / failure path coverage**: I covered abnormal paths
    (exceptions,
    timeouts, out-of-range, division by zero) in addition to the happy path.
    - [ ] **Meaningful & reliable tests**: New tests assert real behavior
    and are
      not written merely to satisfy CI.
    - [ ] **Performance impact**: If this PR touches a performance-sensitive
    path,
      I attached benchmark results and added performance regression tests.
    
